task: scan-step-allocation
agent: opus-optics
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/scan-step
worktree: ../okay-wt-scan-step
spec: docs/benchmarks.md §10 (the per-character breakdown); BACKLOG entry

why: the operator asked what else is worth optimising, and this is the
  repository's own answer: the `Tuple2` that `Scan.step` answers is
  ~19% of lexing's ~171 B per character, the second of three named
  shares. The entry wanted a consumer before an interface change and
  now has several in main sources — RAG's splitter and code chunker,
  Markdown and Yaml, okay-llm's streaming structured parse, and the
  agent's BPE token count on every message.

what: `Scan.stepInto(s, c, out): S` — additive, with a DEFAULT
  delegating to `step`, so every scanner and every caller keeps
  working untouched. The drivers (`all`, `chunks`, `relex`, `stage`)
  and the two direct callers use it; the JSON scanner overrides it and
  defines `step` in terms of it, so there is one implementation. JMH
  with `-prof gc`, because bytes are what this is about.

predict: about a fifth of the allocation on that path and time within
  noise — most characters emit NO token, so the tuple is the only
  thing allocated for them. If the bytes do not move, the change is
  reverted and the number recorded.
